<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Dance · 1859 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dixie</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>Dixie (also &quot;Dixie's Land&quot;, &quot;I Wish I Was in Dixie&quot;)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from 1859 stage shows that mocked Black people; it became the Confederacy's marching song, and many people object to it today.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Daniel Decatur Emmett (authorship disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Daniel Decatur Emmett (authorship disputed; a possible African American origin, through the Snowden family of Ohio, is argued by some scholars)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>blackface minstrel stage of the northern United States; later the Confederacy and white Southerners</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>New York City, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 4 April 1859; published 21 June 1860</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics credited to Daniel Decatur Emmett (d. 1904), published 1860)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Dixie&quot; was written for the blackface minstrel stage, where white performers blacked their faces to caricature enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>. Bryant's Minstrels first performed it as &quot;Dixie's Land&quot; at Mechanics' Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on 4 April 1859, billed as a &quot;plantation song and dance&quot;: a walkaround in which a few minstrels acted out the words and the whole company joined in. Most sources credit the Ohio-born minstrel Daniel Decatur Emmett, who published it with Firth, Pond &amp; Co. on 21 June 1860, though his own accounts of writing it varied and by 1908 no fewer than 37 people had claimed it. Emmett once attributed it to an unnamed Southern Black man, and a Mount Vernon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, tradition links it to the Black musicians of the Snowden family; some scholars accept a Black origin or allow for it, others dismiss it. Its words, sung in a mock Black dialect, give the voice of a freed slave homesick for the plantation, and present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as a positive good: of all the minstrel tunes, it made that case most strongly. Because it was a dance tune, it made light of slavery as well.</p><p>The song became a hit across the country and abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> liked it and it was played during his 1860 campaign. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 1860 it was the hit of the Rumsey and Newcomb Minstrels, and Mrs. John Wood's singing in a burlesque drew seven encores; the publisher P. P. Werlein printed a version without the dialect. Secessionists soon took it as their own. On 20 December 1860 a band played it after each vote for secession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charleston-south-carolina">Charleston, South Carolina</a>, and on 18 February 1861 it was played at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a>'s inauguration as president of the Confederacy. Emmett reportedly said that year that had he known what use it would be put to, he would not have written it. Albert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pike</a> and others wrote war verses, and it was probably the Confederate soldiers' most popular song on the march, in battle and in camp. In the North, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">Frances</a> J. Crosby wrote Union versions, Union bands played it until 1863, and on 10 April 1865, the day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s surrender, Lincoln asked for it at the White House, saying the Union had fairly captured it.</p><p>After the war the song stayed bound to the South and the memory of the Confederacy. In 1905 the United Daughters of the Confederacy campaigned for an official Southern version that would purge its African American associations; none of the 22 entries was adopted. It was played at the dedication of Confederate monuments such as the one in Nashville's Centennial Park in 1909. Black minstrel troupes of the time played it as they arrived in Southern towns; the minstrel Tom Fletcher recalled that it calmed audiences who might otherwise be hostile to a group of Black men. Northern writers used it to signal a Southern setting, and the Black songwriters Eubie Blake and Noble Sissle quoted it in their 1921 musical Shuffle Along.</p><p>From the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> criticised &quot;Dixie&quot; as a racist relic of the Confederacy and a reminder of white domination and segregation, all the more when white opponents of civil rights sang it in answer to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a>&quot;. Students protested it at Southern universities; the University of Miami's president banned it from the band in 1968, members of the 75th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army Band protested it in 1971, and in 1989 three Black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> senators walked out when it was sung in the state's chamber. The musicologist Sam Dennison wrote that it still conjures a South ready to reassert white supremacy. Its defenders call it part of the American patriotic repertoire or of Southern heritage, and some, such as the journalist Clint Johnson and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> state senator Glenn McConnell, called the campaigns against it political correctness or cultural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. In 2016 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> stopped playing it at athletic events after some seven decades. Singers who perform it today usually drop the dialect: René Marie's jazz version sets it beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strange-fruit">Strange Fruit</a>&quot;, Billie Holiday's song about a lynching.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A minstrel-show song, sung in a mock Black dialect, in which the singer longs to be back in the South where he was born. Written as a comic dance, it presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as a happy home for Black people; in its Civil War versions the longing became a defiant vow to stand and fight for the South.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I wish I was in de land ob cotton,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Old times dar am not forgotten;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Dixie Land whar I was born in,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Early on one frosty mornin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Den I wish I was in Dixie, Hooray! Hooray!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Dixie Land, I'll took my stand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To lib an die in Dixie,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Away, Away, Away down south in Dixie,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Away, Away, Away down south in Dixie.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Old Missus marry &quot;Will-de-weaber,&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Willium was a gay deceaber;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But when he put his arm around'er,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He smilled as fierce as a forty-pound'er,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His face was sharp as a butchers cleaber,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But dat did not seem to greab'er;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Old Missus acted de foolish part,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And died for a man dat broke her heart.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now here's a health to the next old Missus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all de galls dat want to kiss us;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But if you want to drive 'way sorrow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Come an hear dis song to-morrow.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dar's buck-wheat cakes an 'Ingen' batter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Makes you fat or a little fatter;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Den hoe it down an scratch your grabble,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To Dixie land I'm bound to trabble.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look away! Look away! Look away! Dixie Land.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Attributed to Daniel Decatur Emmett (1859); the text of the first edition, in its minstrel dialect. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/I_wish_I_was_in_Dixie's_Land" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
