<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The War Song of Dixie (Albert Pike)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new Confederate words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dixie for the Union; Dixie Unionized (Frances J. Crosby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Union answer songs</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bandana Days (Eubie Blake and Noble Sissle, Shuffle Along)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1921</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gone with the Wind (score by Max Steiner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 1930s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dixie (film, Bing Crosby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">biopic of Emmett featuring the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Songs of the South (Norman Luboff Choir)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording used for Southern TV and radio sign-offs</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An American Trilogy (Mickey Newbury)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">combined with Battle Hymn of the Republic and All My Trials</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Civil War (Ken Burns documentary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental versions used</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Dukes of Hazzard</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first twelve notes as the car horn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Appropriation of Cultures (Percival Everett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2004</span><span class="sg-chain-h">referred to in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">René Marie's jazz version</span><span class="sg-chain-h">combined with &quot;Strange Fruit&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">whistling Dixie</span><span class="sg-chain-h">idiom taken from the song</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">De Wild Goose-Nation (Emmett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1844</span><span class="sg-chain-h">anticipates the first part; reworded in the final stanza</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gumbo Chaff</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1830s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the second stanza and of &quot;De Wild Goose-Nation&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bow Wow Wow</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">18th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English song behind &quot;Gumbo Chaff&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Johnny Roach (Emmett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1859</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the chorus follows parts of it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Come Philander Let's Be Marchin</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named by Emmett as a source; only loosely related</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Albany Beef</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Irish-style reel used for the dance</span></li></ul></section>
